 "url": "https://stripe.dev/blog/minions-stripes-one-shot-end-to-end-coding-agents-part-2?read=1",
 "verdicts": {
  "A Stripe devbox is an AWS EC2 instance that contains Stripe's source code and runs services under development; Minions run on the same standard developer environment that Stripe engineers use.": 1,
  "After a Minion pushes, CI runs and auto-applies autofixes for failing tests; failures with no autofix go back to a blueprint agent node for one more local fix attempt.": 1,
  "Because mistakes are confined to the limited blast radius of one devbox, Stripe runs Minions with full permissions and no confirmation prompts.": 1,
  "Blueprints are workflows defined in code that direct a Minion run; each node runs either deterministic code or an agent loop, so the Minion blueprint resembles a state machine mixing deterministic and free-flowing agent nodes.": 1,
  "Devbox warm-up includes cloning large git repositories, warming Bazel and type-checking caches, and starting code generation services; a ready box has a recent copy of master across Stripe's main repos.": 1,
  "Devboxes are 'cattle, not pets': standardized and easy to replace, and many engineers use one devbox per task, sometimes about half a dozen at once.": 1,
  "Stripe aims for a devbox to be ready within 10 seconds by proactively provisioning and warming a pool of devboxes.": 1,
  "Stripe forked Block's coding agent goose in late 2024 and customized it to work within Stripe's LLM infrastructure.": 1,
  "Stripe has an internal security control framework to ensure Minions cannot use their MCP tools to perform destructive actions.": 1,
  "Stripe has over three million tests, which provide feedback to Minions; it is infeasible to run all of them locally, so the standard blueprint includes one iteration against the full CI suite.": 1,
  "Stripe limits CI rounds because CI runs cost tokens, compute and time, and it sees diminishing marginal returns from indefinitely many full CI loops.": 1,
  "Stripe limits Minions to at most two rounds of CI; after the second push and CI run, the branch is sent back to its human operator for manual scrutiny.": 1,
  "Stripe notes that in a large codebase an agent without guidance may struggle to follow best practices or use the proper libraries even with good linters.": 1,
  "Stripe reported that over 1,300 pull requests merged each week are completely minion-produced, up from 1,000 as of Part 1; they are human-reviewed but contain no human-written code.": 1,
  "Stripe says deterministic code for predictable steps, such as always linting at the end of a run, saves tokens and CI costs at scale and gives the agent less opportunity to get things wrong.": 1,
  "Stripe's devboxes run in its QA environment, so Minions have no access to real user data, Stripe's production services, or arbitrary network egress.": 1,
  "The Minion blueprint's agent nodes include 'Implement task' and 'Fix CI failures', while deterministic nodes such as 'Run configured linters' and 'Push changes' invoke no LLM and just run code.": 1
 }
}